@alculquicondor Can we remove `scheduler_goroutines` metric in the next release? Or should we wait more time?

After reviewing everything I believe this is a bit of a red herring.  The root cause is some type of slow down or lock in the system.  The request it self isn't fatal and maybe adds a bit more overhead but it would be pretty minimal I think.

When this error happens it will fail then re-queue accordingly https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/cf18d80d035780739575b8cc889e8f26bf36779f/pkg/kubelet/pod_workers.go#L1070-L1084

For windows there seems to be two cases:

1. the system is slow due to resource contention - in this case it might time out in kubelet but still be creating in containerd.  In this case, when it get around to trying again it will find the container started and everything goes on working (this is the case in https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/107561#issuecomment-1013516103 although the pod fails for other reasons later)
2. Something breaks in containerd getting stuck (in this case timeouts in OS due to resource contention) - in this case it will retry until things get cleaned up proceed onward.  There does seem to be a bug in Windows containerd where container startup times out then task creation doesn't work properly and then deletion fails.  

The second one for Windows happen in https://prow.k8s.io/view/gs/kubernetes-jenkins/pr-logs/pull/kubernetes-sigs_cluster-api-provider-azure/1932/pull-cluster-api-provider-azure-windows-containerd-upstream-with-ci-artifacts/1478533581516050432

